    {
      "filename": "Modern Manchuria -- Political.jpg",
      "title": "File:Modern Manchuria -- Political.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Modern_Manchuria_--_Political.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modern_Manchuria_--_Political.jpg",
      "width": 3365,
      "height": 5217,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Albert Herrmann &amp; Georg Westermann",
      "description": "\"Modern Manchuria—Political\", a map of Manchuria (Northeastern China: Heilongjiang, Jilin, &amp; Liaoning Provinces and northeastern Inner Mongolia) under the Japanese-aligned Manchukuo Empire (\"Empire of Manchoutikuo\") just before the onset of the Second Sino-Japanese War (World War II). Inset map of Mukden (now Shenyang in Liaoning).\nThe Chinese provinces of Heilongjiang (\"Helungkiang\"), Jilin (\"Kirin\"), and Liaoning are marked in black, as is the Guandong Leased Territory (\"Kuan-tung\") held directly by the Empire of Japan. The districts of the Manchukuo Empire (\"recognized only by Japan\") are marked separately in red: Antung (Andong), Fengtien (Fengtian), Chinchou, Jehol (Rehe), Khingan's North, East, and South Sections, Heiho (Heihe), Sankiang (Sanjiang), Lungliang, Pinkiang, Kirin (Jilin), and Chintao.\n\nThe Willow Palisade marked, as is a sliver of the eastern Great Wall. Provincial capitals and county seats (\"district capitals\") noted, along with rough populations (&gt;100k or &lt;100k), Catholic archdioceses, Protestant mission stations, Tibetan Buddhist monasteries, treaty ports, and foreign concessions. Roads are distinguished as \"trunk motoring roads\" or \"secondary roads\". \n\n\nFor a guide to other typography, symbols, etc., see \"HCAC Scripts Symbols &amp; Abbreviations.jpg\".",
      "dateCreated": "1935",
